'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are working on a biology experiment-design task (BioDiscoveryAgent-style perturbation search). Your goal is to implement a better selection policy to identify genes (or gene pairs) whose perturbation yields extreme outcomes on a dataset-specific score.\n\n## High-Level Goal (Perturbation Search)\nThere is a fixed library of perturbations (genes for most datasets; gene pairs for some datasets).\nIn each round, the policy must select a batch of NEW perturbations (no repeats). An oracle reveals the measured score and whether the perturbation is a "hit" under a dataset-specific hit definition.\n\nThe evaluation score is based on selecting strong perturbations under a limited query budget (default: 5 rounds × 128 per round = 640 queries), using metrics like:\n- cumulative_hits\n- normalized cumulative gain (ncg)\n\nYou may use any suitable algorithmic model to design the selection policy, including traditional algorithms, machine learning models, deep learning models, biological models, and mathematical models.\n\n## What You Are Allowed To Change (ONLY MODEL)\nDo NOT modify anything except the model implementation file.\nDo NOT change any function signatures or IO behavior.\n\n## Policy Interface (MUST KEEP EXACTLY)\nYour model code must provide one of the following:\n1) A module-level function:\n   select(candidates, history, batch_size, seed) -> list[int]\nor\n2) A class with a select(...) method (class name can be SelectionPolicy / Policy / Model).\n\nThe evaluator will call select(...) each round.\n\n## Input Semantics\ncandidates:\n- A list of perturbations (full search space).\n- Each entry is a dict. For single-gene datasets it includes: {gene: <HGNC>}.\n- For pair datasets it includes: {gene_a: <HGNC>, gene_b: <HGNC>}.\n\nhistory:\n- A list of previously queried entries, each including:\n  - candidate_index: int (index into candidates)\n  - gene / gene_a+gene_b identifiers\n  - score: float (oracle measured score)\n  - hit: int (0/1) if enabled\n\n## Optional Tool: Gene Search (Achilles)\nWhen enabled by the runner (`BDA_GENE_SEARCH=1` and `BDA_CSV_PATH` points to a directory containing `achilles.csv`),\nyou may import and call:\n\n- `import bda_tools`\n- `bda_tools.gene_search(query_gene: str, k: int = 10, diverse: bool = False) -> list[int]`\n\nThis returns candidate indices for similar (or diverse) genes using Achilles features.\n\nbatch_size:\n- The number of NEW perturbations to select each round (default: 128).\n\nseed:\n- An integer seed that may change each round.\n\n## Constraints\n- Do not attempt to read the ground-truth CSV files directly.\n- Do not return duplicates or already-selected indices; if you do, they will be ignored and replaced.\n- The policy should run fast and be robust even when history is small (including empty).\n\n## Practical Tip\nBefore writing or revising `select(...)`, first inspect the previous run\'s result file if it is available:\n- `harness/outputs/metrics.json`\n- `metrics.test.queried_history` contains what was selected and the observed outcomes.\n\nUse the observed previous-round results to guide the next policy. For example, identify high-scoring or hit genes, low-scoring genes, repeated patterns, promising gene families, or failed regions of the search space before choosing the next candidates.\n\nWhen gene search is enabled, you may choose some promising genes from the previous results and call `bda_tools.gene_search(...)` to find similar or diverse candidate genes, then combine those candidates with other model-based or exploration-based choices.\nYou are the Evolution Proposer for an AI4S (AI for Science) project.\n\nYour job: Propose ONE targeted modification to the project code or configuration based on evolution history to improve the benchmark score.\n\n## Hardware\n- CPU only. Do NOT use CUDA/GPU-specific code.\n\n## Your Workspace\n- Evolution history: /inspire/qb-ilm/project/cq-scientific-cooperation-zone/public/ywang/meta-harness-ev